1. Introduction

   This file contains instructions for gaining access to the required
   cmake executable and the lapack and blas libraries and the optional
   git and doxygen executables and accessing, building,
   installing, and testing FreeEOS using those prerequisites.

   In all cases the prerequisites are popular executables and
   libraries so with any luck at all they are already installed on
   your platform.  For rpm-based Linux distributions try the "rpm -qi
   <packagename>" command and for Debian-based Linux systems try the
   "dpkg --list |grep <packagename>" commands to find whether any
   given <packagename> (such as "cmake") is already installed on your
   system.  For Unix systems consult with your system administrator
   and/or use the "find" command to look for cmake, etc., on your
   system.

   If you do not have these packages installed on Debian-based
   systems, then simply issue "apt-get install cmake liblapack-dev
   libblas-dev git doxygen" to install.  (Installing these development
   versions of liblapack and libblas will automatically also install
   other required packages.) On rpm-based Linux systems search the web
   for appropriate rpm's for your particular version of Linux. For
   example, enter cmake, liblapack.so, libblas.so, git, or doxygen in the
   search box at <http://rpm.pbone.net/> to find rpms for those
   software components for a wide variety of Linux distribution
   versions.  Make sure you download and install _only_ the rpms that
   are appropriate for your Linux distribution version.

2. CMake Installation Prerequisite

   FreeEOS uses a CMake-based build system which requires that you use
   CMake version 3.18.4 or greater.  Most software platforms make this
   popular build-system tool available as an optional install, see
   comments above. But if that version or greater of CMake is not
   available on your platform, then you should build the latest
   version of CMake (3.23.1 at this time of writing) following the
   directions in <https://cmake.org/install/>.

3. lapack and blas Installation Prerequisites

   FreeEOS solves systems of linear equations with the aid of the
   lapack and blas libraries.  Therefore, the FreeEOS build requires
   that you have the lapack and blas libraries installed somewhere on
   your system.  Most software platforms make these popular libraries
   available as an optional install, see comments above.  For those
   rare systems which do not have official packages for the lapack and
   blas libraries that you can install, then you must build lapack and
   blas following the latest instructions at
   <https://github.com/Reference-LAPACK/lapack/>.  I have done such a
   build recently using the CMake-based build instructions that I
   found were really easy to follow so that is the method I would
   recommend for this case.

4. git optional prerequisite to help access the latest version of the FreeEOS source code

   [git](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Git) is the most widely used
   and widely available version control system.  Therefore, most
   software platforms make this popular tool available as an optional
   install, see comments above.  For those rare systems which do not
   have an official package for git, there is always the option of
   building git for yourself, but I haven't ever needed to do that
   myself so I cannot comment on how easy that is.  In any case, git
   is an optional prerequisite.  That is, those who do not have access
   to it will not be able to access the git versions of FreeEOS
   (typically useful for testing the last few commits before a release
   as well as all commits corresponding to an official release) and
   will have to use the traditional method instead (unpacking a
   release tarball) to gain access to just the official releases of
   FreeEOS.

5. doxygen optional prerequisite to help provide a nice form of
   documentation for FreeEOS

   [doxygen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doxygen) is a widely used
   documentation generator which relies on a specially formatted code
   commentary to generate a nice form of documentation of Fortran (and other) source code.
   I have used doxygen to provide summary documentation of
   all FreeEOS modules and procedures and parameter documentation
   of a subset of the procedures representing a work in progress
   to document the parameters of all FreeEOS procedures.
   Instructions are given below
   for generating (with doxygen) and accessing the resulting
   documentation for those routines.

6. Obtain access to the FreeEOS source code.

   * Traditional method

     Download an official FreeEOS release tarball from
     <https://sourceforge.net/projects/freeeos/files/freeeos/> and
     unpack it.

   * git method (only available if you have installed git, see above).

     Here is the git command you should use to install a local git
     repository for FreeEOS that tracks every FreeEOS commit since
     version 1.2.0 (which was released in 2005).  Before running this
     command you should execute "ls free_eos.git" to confirm that
     directory does not exist before it is generated by the following
     command *which should only be executed once*.

git clone https://git.code.sf.net/p/freeeos/freeeos free_eos.git

     Once that command succeeds, here are some some typical basic git
     commands I run which you might want to try as well.

# Move to the free_eos.git subdirectory containing your local git
# repository for FreeEOS for all further git commands
cd free_eos.git

# Get help with the git tag command (which gives you access to prior versions of FreeEOS if you like)
git help tag

# List all prior tags
git tag --list

# Get help with the git checkout command
git help checkout

# checkout version 2.2.1 of the source code, look around there,
# then checkout the 3.0.0 release and look around there to
# see all the changes between the two
git checkout v2_2_1
ls src |less
git checkout free_eos-3.0.0
ls src |less

# Do the same for any git changes since 3.0.0
git checkout master
ls src |less

# Suppose the master version has been updated (by some development push
# by me) since you cloned it.  Here is how you gain access to that latest
# push which will reside at the tip of the master branch of FreeEOS.

git checkout master
git pull

# Look at the commit messages for the most recent changes on git master branch
# including, presumably my latest push.
git log --name-status

   The above commands are probably more than you need to know about
   git in order to access any version of FreeEOS from my latest
   development push back through more than 15 years.

   N.B. In general I do extensive testing of each of my git pushes and
   that is especially true of official releases.  However, such
   testing may still miss some issues so if you find such an issue,
   please get in immediate contact with me on the [freeeos-general
   mailing
   list](https://sourceforge.net/projects/freeeos/lists/freeeos-general)
   since I would prefer such issues to be solved sooner rather than later.

7. Configure the FreeEOS build

   * Set an environment variable to help cmake find libraries that implement
     the BLAS and LAPACK API's.  For example, suppose you had libblas.a and
     liblapack.a installed in /home/myusername/mylibs. Then set

export CMAKE_LIBRARY_PATH=/home/myusername/mylibs

     to help CMake find those versions.  Note, the above command is
     optional if the directory is a standard install location (e.g.,
     /usr/lib where cmake should need no help to find it).

   * Set the FC environment variable to help choose the compiler. FreeEOS
     has been thoroughly tested with gfortran, ifort, and nagfor.  So to
     choose one of those put the desired compiler on the PATH and
     specify one of

FC=gfortran
FC=ifort
FC=nagfor

   * Set the FFLAGS environment variable to choose the appropriate flags for
     the chosen compiler and desired type of build, test, and install.
     ________________________________________

     NOTES CONCERNING COMPILER FLAGS

     + gfortran (simple build and install)

FFLAGS='-O3'

       This high degree of optimization has proved to work well with
       gfortran and FreeEOS.

     + gfortran (build, test, and install)

FFLAGS='-O3 -std=f2008 -Wall -Wunused-parameter -Wimplicit-interface -Wuse-without-only -Wconversion-extra -Wno-unused-dummy-argument -Wno-maybe-uninitialized -finit-real=snan -finit-integer=2147483647'

       These gfortran options mean the following:

       * use high optimization (-O3);

       * check there are no violations of the Fortran 2008 standard
         (-std=f2008);

       * check for all standard gfortran warnings that should be easy
         to avoid (-Wall);

       * check there are no unused Fortran parameters specified by the
         code (-Wunused-parameter);

       * warn if a procedure is called without an explicit interface
         (-Wimplicit-interface);

       * warn if a 'USE' statement has no 'ONLY' qualifier
         (-Wuse-without-only);

       * warn about implicit conversions between different types and
         kinds (-Wconversion-extra);

       * do not warn about unused dummy arguments since those will be
         dealt with after this release (-Wno-unused-dummy-argument);

       * do not warn about possible uninitialized code discovered
         through static code analysis because this gfortran option is
         simplistically implemented so it generates way too many false
         positives and dynamic run-time searches for uninitialized
         variables via tainting is much more reliable
         (-Wno-maybe-uninitialized);

       * taint reals with signalling NaN's (-finit-real=snan); and

       * taint integers with the huge value for the default 4-byte
         integers used by gfortran (-finit-integer=2147483647).

       N.B. for gfortran -finit-real and -finit-integer are
       implemented only in a partial way so that, for example,
       allocated arrays and equivalenced arrays are specifically not
       tainted by these options.  Therefore, I have added workaround
       code to make up for this gfortran deficiency.  That code is
       configured with the CMake options
       -Dif_taint_allocated_real=.true. -Dif_taint_allocated_integer=.true.
       (which should only be used with gfortran) to taint allocated
       and equivalanced real and integer arrays with a signalling NaN
       for the real case and the huge value (2147483647) for the
       default integer case consistent with but supplementing the
       above -finit-real and -finit-integer options.

     + ifort (simple build and install)

FFLAGS='-O3'

       This high degree of optimization has proved to work well with
       ifort and FreeEOS.

     + ifort (build, test, and install)

FFLAGS='-O3 -std08 -warn all -init=snan,huge'

       These ifort options mean the following:

       * use high optimization (-O3);

       * check there are no violations of the Fortran 2008 standard
         (-std08);

       * check for all standard ifort warnings that should be easy to
         avoid (-warn all);

       * taint all reals with signalling NaN's and all integers with
         huge (for the default kind of integer used by FreeEOS).

         N.B. ifort discussion and documentation implies these
         tainting methods to help find uninitialized issues are not
         nearly as limited as in the gfortran case so this option
         should required no additional help (i.e., cmake should be
         configured with -Dif_taint_allocated_real=.false.
         -Dif_taint_allocated_integer=.false.).

     + nagfor (simple build and install)

FFLAGS='-O3'

       This high degree of optimization has proved to work well with
       nagfor and FreeEOS.

     + nagfor (build, standard test, and install)

FFLAGS='-O3 -f2008 -w=uda'

       These nagfor options mean the following:

       * use high optimization (-O3);

       * check there are no violations of the Fortran 2008 standard
         (-f2008); and

       * by default nagfor warns about everything so it is necessary
         to suppress warning messages about unused dummy arguments for
         the same reason as in the gfortran case (-wuda).

     N.B. By default nagfor generates many more code warnings then in
     the gfortran -Wall or ifort -warn all cases.  For this release
     the Fortran code has been updated to eliminate all "Warning:" and
     "Extension(NAG):" type of warnings made by the nagfor compiler,
     but some ~50 "Questionable:" type of warnings remain and the plan
     is to address most of those after the present release.

     + nagfor (build, non-standard -C=all test, and install)

FFLAGS="-C=all -f2008 -w=uda -gline"

       These nagfor options mean the following:

       * Perform all ~10 run-time tests possible with nagfor except
         (see below) checking for uninitialized variables (-C=all).

       * Compile code to produce a traceback when a runtime error
         message is generated (-gline).

       * check there are no violations of the Fortran 2008 standard
         (-f2008); and

       * suppress warning messages about unused dummy arguments for
        same reason as in the gfortran case (-wuda).

       N.B. I consistently found with this test no runtime issues at
       all for the usual simple_test, valgrind_test,
       comprehensive_test and FreeEOS_papers test targets.
       Furthermore, despite the lack of optimization the results for
       this test were bit-identical with the results from the same
       test targets for the standard nagfor test.

     + nagfor (build, non-standard -C=undefined test)

FFLAGS="-C=undefined -f2008 -w=uda -gline"

       These nagfor options mean the following:

       * Perform a run-time test for uninitialized variables
         (-C=undefined).

       * Compile code to produce a traceback when a runtime error
         message is generated (-gline).

       * check there are no violations of the Fortran 2008 standard
         (-f2008); and

       * suppress warning messages about unused dummy arguments for
         same reason as in the gfortran case (-wuda).

       N.B. according to the nagfor man page there are some
       constraints on the Fortran code which can be analyzed with
       -C=undefined.  As far as I know the only one of those
       constraints that is possibly relevant is

       "Certain intrinsic functions require the entirety of their
       arguments to be defined, even if some portions are not actually
       required for the value of the function.  For example, the PAD
       argument to RESHAPE when no padding is actually required, and
       elements of the ARRAY argument to PACK that correspond to false
       elements of the MASK."

       And even in this case, I believe RESHAPE is only used to help
       define parameter arrays which is all done at compile time
       rather than run time.  In any case (and as expected from the
       documentation of these constraints) there don't seem to be any
       build warnings about such constraints being violated.

       However, the actual nagfor implementation of -C=undefined
       appears to have problems.  For example, any attempt at
       optimization with -O2 or -O3 combined with -C=undefined
       generates peculiar build errors concerning character functions
       that don't seem to exist (!).  And with no -O option (as above)
       most test executables do not complete normally and instead
       produce segfaults (!)

       Nevertheless, despite these issues the build of the test
       targets below did generate Runtime Errors for the following two
       types of innocuous uninitialized issues before the segfault
       occurred:

       1. A.and.B where A is false so A.and.B must be false regardless
          of uninitialized values in the B calculation when A is
          false.  I am pretty sure (since gfortran tainting did not
          find any of these) that gfortran simply evaluates logical
          expressions from left to right until the true or false
          nature of the result is determined.  ifort found some of
          these (in particular one case where the uninitialized
          calculation in B had a bad side effect), but since nagfor
          found so many more of them I think nagfor -C=undefined goes
          out of its way to evaluate all parts of logical expressions.

        2. Copy of undefined quantity that won't subsequently be used.
           gfortran and ifort experience indicates tainting alone does
           not generate an invalid floating-point exception for this
           copying case.  But since nagfor -C-undefined found such
           issues, I assume that implementation does something extra
           such as adding 0._fp_kind to the result of the copy (which
           will generate an invalid floating-point exception).

        N.B. my best guess from the above results (and their
        completeness relative to what tainting reveals in the gfortran
        and ifort cases for the same tests) is the segfault occurs
        late in the execution.  For example, it may be associated with
        all the library stuff that happens when the standard stop
        statement is executed.  But I have not had time to pursue this
        issue with the nagfor developers to find out what is really
        going on.

     END OF NOTES CONCERNING COMPILER FLAGS
     ________________________________________

   * Create an empty build directory.

mkdir build_dir

   * Configure a FreeEOS build in that empty build tree.

cd build_dir
# Make sure that directory is empty before you use it for your build
ls

# Set your installation prefix in the following shell (bash) variable.
# Typically this should be a location where your user account has
# write access.

# This is just an example installation prefix I use, and you will
# presumably want to use something different.
FreeEOS_INSTALL_PREFIX=/home/irwin/cdburn1/interior_eos/install/git

# Set the location of the top-level directory of the source tree in
# the following shell (bash) variable.  In this case I have assumed
# you created the empty build_dir as a subdirectory of that top-level
# directory, but if not, you must set SOURCE_TREE to the absolute path
# of that top-level directory.
SOURCE_TREE=..

# Configure the build of FreeEOS and make sure that configuration worked.
cmake -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$FreeEOS_INSTALL_PREFIX -DBUILD_TEST=ON $SOURCE_TREE >& cmake.out
less cmake.out

   See the CMakeCache.txt file generated by the above command for
   documentation of all FreeEOS build-system options.  For example if
   you have doxygen installed (see optional prerequisite above) and
   you also specify the -DBUILD_DOX_DOC=ON cmake option then the
   doxygen form of html AND PDF documentation is automatically built
   and installed.

   On Linux the above cmake command prepares the Makefiles in
   build_dir so that subsequent make steps are able to build, install
   and optionally test FreeEOS.  But other non-make options for the
   CMake back end system are available as well.

8. Build FreeEOS

make VERBOSE=1 >& make.out
less make.out

9. Install FreeEOS

   Note this installation will use the installation prefix you set
   above using the shell (bash) variable $FreeEOS_INSTALL_PREFIX.  For
   example, if doxygen is installed and you set -DBUILD_DOX_DOC=ON
   (see configuration step above), then as a result of building the
   install target below, the doxygen html and PDF documentation generated in
   the above build step is installed in the html and latex subdirectories of
   $FreeEOS_INSTALL_PREFIX/share/doc/free_eos/doxygen, and you
   should be able to use your browser to look at those results.  And
   similarly for other parts of FreeEOS such as libraries (installed
   in $FreeEOS_INSTALL_PREFIX/lib by building the install target) and
   executables (installed in $FreeEOS_INSTALL_PREFIX/bin by building
   the install target) that were built in the above build step.

make VERBOSE=1 install >& make_install.out
less make_install.out

10. (Optional) test FreeEOS
    Use the methods discussed in README.testing.

11. Link your own executables with the installed version of FreeEOS

    Once you are satisfied with your build and install of FreeEOS you
    will want to use this library yourself by linking your own
    applications to it.  The best guide in this case is the
    self-contained tutorial example located at
    cmake/test_FreeEOS_install which illustrates both a CMake-based
    build example and a "raw" (i.e., non-CMake-based) build example of
    how to build and link an application to the installed FreeEOS.
    See cmake/test_FreeEOS_install/README for details concerning this
    tutorial example.
